---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize or Compress Image to 50 KB Online | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Compress a photo or scan to 50 KB or less in your browser, without uploading it. Learn when 50 KB is enough, how to avoid a blurry result and what to check.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize or compress image to 50 KB', '/resize-image-to-50kb/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/resize-image-to-50kb/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize or compress an image to 50 KB" lead="The tool below starts with a 50 KB maximum already selected. Add a photo or a scan, check the preview and download the result. Change the numbers if your form asks for something different." />

  <ImageTool client:load defaultMaxKb={50} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool compresses your picture inside your browser, so it needs JavaScript. Turn it on and reload to get a
      file under 50 KB. The advice below on what fits in 50 KB is useful with any other editor as well.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>Where 50 KB sits between the common limits</h2>
    <p>
      Forms tend to pick one of a few round numbers. Ten and twenty kilobytes force a very small picture, and a hundred
      leaves comfortable room. Fifty is the middle case, and it is where the choice between a photo and a document
      matters most. A cropped head-and-shoulders portrait usually survives 50 KB with a clear face. A full page scan with
      small print often does not, unless you accept fewer pixels and a plainer picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are not sure which limit your form really means, compare it with the pages for{' '}
      <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">20 KB</a> and <a href="/resize-image-to-100kb/">100 KB</a>. They show what
      you gain or give up on either side of this one.
    </p>

    <h2>Decide first: portrait, document or signature</h2>
    <p>
      The same 50 KB budget is spent differently depending on what is in the picture, so decide which of these you
      have before you press anything.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A portrait or ID-style photo.</strong> Smooth skin and a plain backdrop compress well. Crop tight and
        let the tool shrink the pixel size; faces stay readable at modest dimensions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A document, certificate or marksheet.</strong> Thin black text on white is harsh on JPEG, which leaves
        grey fuzz around letters. Keep more pixels, accept a lower quality number and read the preview at full size
        before you download.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A signature.</strong> Use the <a href="/resize-signature/">signature tool</a>. It crops to the ink and
        puts it on white, which saves far more than quality changes do.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Two ways to reach 50 KB, and when to use each</h2>
    <p>
      A file shrinks when it holds fewer pixels or when each pixel is stored with less detail. The tool can lean on
      either. Quality-first keeps your pixel size and lowers the compression quality until the file fits, which suits a
      form that fixes the width and height. Dimensions-first keeps quality reasonable and reduces the pixels, which
      suits a form that names only a size in KB. For a photo that started life at several megabytes, dimensions-first
      often looks cleaner at 50 KB, because pushing quality to the floor is what produces blocky patches.
    </p>

    <h2>What to look for in the preview</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Zoom to full size on the eyes and the edge of the hair. Blocks and smearing show there first.</li>
      <li>Check any printed text, such as a name on a badge or the small lines of a certificate.</li>
      <li>Look at flat areas such as a wall or sky for banding, which appears when quality is pushed too low.</li>
      <li>Read the reported size and pixel dimensions, and confirm they match what your form states.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Forms that give a range such as 20 KB to 50 KB</h2>
    <p>
      Some portals reject files that are too small as well as too large. Enter both numbers in the tool. If the picture
      lands under your minimum, the tool raises the quality to store more detail. A very plain image can still end up
      small, and the tool tells you so instead of padding the file with filler bytes that a portal might strip.
    </p>

    <h2>If the portal still says the file is too large</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Counting differs.</strong> Some sites count 1 KB as 1,000 bytes and others as 1,024. SizeTune aims
        under the 1,000-byte count, so the file passes either way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The file was changed after saving.</strong> Sending it through a chat app or an email client can
        re-encode it. Upload the file from the folder where you saved it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other limits apply.</strong> The portal may also cap pixel width and height or accept only certain
        formats. Use the <a href="/check-image-size/">image checker</a> to test a file against all of those rules before
        you submit.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about 50 KB images</h2>
    <h3>Is 50 KB enough for a clear photo?</h3>
    <p>
      For a cropped portrait, usually yes. For a full-frame picture with lots of detail, you will see a loss. Crop
      first and compare the preview with your original.
    </p>
    <h3>What is the difference between compressing and resizing?</h3>
    <p>
      Compressing lowers the file size by storing less detail. Resizing changes the pixel dimensions. This page does
      both as needed to land under your limit, and you choose which one the tool favours.
    </p>
    <h3>Will it work for PNG or WebP files?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes. You can pick the output format in the tool, though WebP output depends on your browser supporting it. JPEG is usually the smallest for photographs, and many forms
      require it. If your picture is a WebP or PNG, you can also use the{' '}
      <a href="/webp-to-jpg/">WebP to JPG</a> or <a href="/png-to-jpg/">PNG to JPG</a> pages.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I do several pictures at once?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes. Add more than one file and download the results together as a single ZIP. Each picture is still checked
      against your limit on its own.
    </p>
    <h3>Does my picture leave my device?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The work happens in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for what is and is not
      collected.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/resize-image-to-50kb/" />
</Base>
